                {
                  "id": 9311,
                  "author": 7101,
                  "authorName": "Sam Student",
                  "subject": "Nernst equation question",
                  "message": "<p>Why does the potassium equilibrium potential get less negative when extracellular K+ goes up?</p>",
                  "hoursAgo": 30
                },
                {
                  "id": 9312,
                  "parent": 9311,
                  "author": 7001,
                  "authorName": "Dr Ada Demo",
                  "subject": "Re: Nernst equation question",
                  "message": "<p>Hi Sam, good question. With more K+ outside, the concentration gradient driving K+ out is smaller, so less charge separation is needed to balance it. Best, Ada</p><p>(Demo text, fake.)</p>",
                  "hoursAgo": 27
                },
                {
                  "id": 9313,
                  "parent": 9312,
                  "author": 7101,
                  "authorName": "Sam Student",
                  "subject": "Re: Nernst equation question",
                  "message": "<p>That makes sense, thank you!</p>",
                  "hoursAgo": 26
                }
              ]
            },
